# Aligning the Burger ontology to Brick (Day 40).
#
# Kept in its own file: burger.ttl stays usable without Brick, and anyone who wants both
# loads this file too. This follows Brick's guide to extensions
# (https://docs.brickschema.org/extra/extending.html): your own namespace, your own terms,
# and links that say how each relates to an existing Brick term.
#
# Pick the WEAKEST link that is true:
#   rdfs:subClassOf     every burger X is also a brick Y (but not the other way round)
#   owl:equivalentClass X and Y mean exactly the same; a reasoner merges their members
#   skos:exactMatch     same meaning, said for people and search tools (no OWL logic)
#   skos:closeMatch     similar, NOT the same; no logic at all, a pointer for readers
@prefix brick:  <https://brickschema.org/schema/Brick#> .
@prefix burger: <https://example.org/burger#> .
@prefix owl:    <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:   <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

# Every piece of burger equipment is building equipment. Not every Brick equipment is ours.
burger:Equipment rdfs:subClassOf brick:Equipment .
burger:Point rdfs:subClassOf brick:Point .
burger:Kitchen rdfs:subClassOf brick:Room .

# A temperature sensor is a temperature sensor: truly the same meaning.
burger:TemperatureSensor owl:equivalentClass brick:Temperature_Sensor ;
    skos:exactMatch brick:Temperature_Sensor .

# Our relationships are special cases of Brick's.
burger:hasPoint rdfs:subPropertyOf brick:hasPoint .
burger:hasLocation rdfs:subPropertyOf brick:hasLocation .

# A restaurant is a business, a building is a structure. A food-court burger stand is a
# restaurant but not a building. Similar, not the same: closeMatch, and no logic.
burger:Restaurant skos:closeMatch brick:Building .
